                    round1_title:        `Round 1 — Crisis Assessment`,
                    round1_desc:         `<b>April 2026 — Four simultaneous crises hit Expediciones Montaña Colombia.</b><br><br><b>Crisis 1:</b> The Ministry of Environment restricts access to La Calera Park (60% of revenue). Groups reduced from 2/day to 1/day and from 12 to 6 people. Estimated loss: COP 3,456M annually.<br><br><b>Crisis 2:</b> New environmental requirements demand guide certification, ultralight equipment, and carbon footprint system. Cost: COP 124M.<br><br><b>Crisis 3:</b> Viator and foreign operators like Adventure Brasil erode the local brand. Direct bookings drop from 40% to 20%.<br><br><b>Crisis 4:</b> A competitor (Aventuras Sierra) contaminates Gorgona Park. The Ministry announces audits for all operators within 60 days.<br><br><b>What weighs on each option:</b> in 2025 the 24 guides led trips worth COP 6,912M, about COP 72M per guide per quarter, and a quarter cannot bring in more revenue than the active guides can lead. A guide who is let go does not come back this year: training one takes years. Cost reduction means letting guides go: each one takes about COP 35M of the investment and saves about COP 20M a quarter, and the operation loses about 5% of its revenue. The base expansion plan (COP 150M) covers the routes the operations director found in parks the restriction does not touch; each peso invested beyond that plan opens routes that bring in half as much as the planned ones. Clients brought by an international partner arrive like bookings through an intermediary, with a thinner margin, and how many it brings depends on what the company invests in the alliance: each peso beyond COP 150M brings half as many as the ones before.<br><br>Select a strategy and investment level for this quarter.`,
